#!/root/agent/venv/bin/python
"""Is every competitor Actor handle we name in public copy still ON the Store? (cycle 2844)

WHY THIS EXISTS -- the gap c2842/c2843 asked a QUALITY slot to close, measured.
`bin/check-competitor-claims` already live-resolves handles and already distinguishes an
undisclosed dead listing from one the prose already discloses, so c2843 reused it as a
fleet-wide delisting sweep. That run found exactly 1 undisclosed dead handle and read as
"the fleet is clean". It is not: check-competitor-claims only ever resolves a handle that
sits inside a USER-COUNT claim (`USERS.finditer`), so a rival named only in a PRICE or
FEATURE sentence -- the large majority -- is never liveness-checked by anything.

Measured on 2026-11-07 across `actors/*/README.md` + `site/content/blog/*.md`:
  2,839 unique backticked `owner/slug` handles fleet-wide
    918 inside a user-count claim  (the only ones check-competitor-claims resolves)
  1,921 never liveness-checked by any tool  (68%)
The same shape is what c2842 hit by hand: `steam-reviews-scraper`'s README names 110
unique handles but only 6 of them carry a user count, and hand-checking all 110 found
7 confirmed 404s. check-competitor-claims could not have found those 7 -- it never looked.

So this is NOT a second clock on the same work (the bin/checks-due `_intervals` warning
about double-booking): it is the other 68% of the handles, and it is deliberately a
separate tool rather than a bolt-on because the two checks answer different questions at
different cost. check-competitor-claims resolves ~900 idents AND reads prose guards for a
possible --fix; this one resolves ~2,800 idents and writes nothing, ever.

WHAT A FINDING MEANS. A delisted rival is not a cosmetic staleness: our READMEs compare
our price and fields against named Actors, and a comparison against a listing that no
longer exists is a marketing claim we cannot back up (the cycle-388 reason
check-competitor-claims exists at all). The fix is never "delete the sentence" -- it is
the standard inline note the fleet already uses, which keeps the history honest:
    (**delisted — the Apify API returned 404 for this Actor on YYYY-MM-DD**)
plus past-tensing the surrounding claim and correcting any count ("four undercutters")
that silently included the dead one.

OUTPUT (one line per file:line:handle, deduped):
  UNDISCLOSED <file>:<line>  `owner/slug` -- 404, no removal notice near the mention
  SHARED      <file>:<line>  `owner/slug` -- 404, notice nearby but another handle intervenes
  DISCLOSED   <file>:<line>  `owner/slug` -- 404, prose already discloses it
  URL-LIKE    `owner/slug` -- 404 on a domain-shaped owner, read as a URL not a handle
  ANOMALY     `owner/slug` -- HTTP <code> twice (NOT treated as delisted)
UNDISCLOSED is work. An ANOMALY is an API problem, never evidence of a removal -- c2842
saw 0 of these across 110 handles and c1145/c1148/c1159 each saw a transient non-200 on a
listing that was still live, which is why every non-200 is retried once.

DISCLOSURE TEST is copied deliberately from check-competitor-claims (cycles 2091/2092),
including its adjacency rule: a removal notice in the window counts only if no OTHER
backticked `owner/slug` comes between the handle and the notice, because these paragraphs
name rivals back-to-back inside one sentence and a bare window test says "disclosed" for
any dead handle sitting one dead neighbour away.

SHARED is this tool's own addition, forced by the first smoke run (c2844,
`steam-reviews-scraper`): our prose also writes GROUP disclosures -- "the two future-dated
listings flagged below (`luminar/...`; `entrogix_works/...`) no longer resolve on the
Store -- a direct API fetch on both returns `404`" -- where the notice genuinely covers
both handles but each handle has the other one between it and the notice. Strict
adjacency calls those UNDISCLOSED, which would send a future cycle to re-fix two already
honest paragraphs. Loosening adjacency instead (e.g. "ignore intervening handles that are
also 404") would re-open exactly the c2092 false-DISCLOSED hole, so neither bucket gets
to absorb the case: it is reported separately and read by hand once. Keep it that way.

KNOWN_NON_HANDLES / the skip rules exist because backticked `a/b` is also how these docs
write file paths (`bin/_unit_price.py`) and URLs (`api.github.com/rate_limit`). Those
404 like a dead Actor does. Extensions and repo dirs are filtered structurally; anything
else that turns out to be a URL rather than a handle gets ONE hand-added line in
KNOWN_NON_HANDLES, the same convention check-competitor-claims uses for COMPETITORS.
Do NOT widen this into a dots-in-owner heuristic: `a.jonzy`, `george.the.developer`,
`insight.solutions` and `caffein.dev` are all real Apify usernames in our copy today.

Usage:
  bin/check-handle-liveness                 # fleet sweep -- the run of record
  bin/check-handle-liveness --only steam    # partial, repeatable; NOT a run of record
  bin/check-handle-liveness --save          # also write state/handle_liveness.json
Read-only on every doc. Record the sweep the same cycle you run it:
  bin/checks-due --ran check-handle-liveness --note "..."
"""
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import time
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor

import httpx

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
OURS = "fetchsmith"
WORKERS = 8
WINDOW = 400  # chars after the handle, same as check-competitor-claims' disclosure window

# Full backtick content only, so ordinary prose and partial paths are never matched.
# Owner charset matches check-competitor-claims' USERS regex so the two tools agree on
# what a handle looks like.
HANDLE = re.compile(r"`([a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{2,})/([A-Za-z0-9_.-]+)`")
# Every phrasing our own READMEs actually use for a removal. check-competitor-claims
# matches only "gone from the Store"; the c2844 smoke run found three more live in the
# fleet ("delisted — the Apify API returned 404 ...", the c2842 standard note;
# "confirmed 404"; "no longer resolve on the Store ... returns `404
# record-or-token-not-found`"), and a disclosure the checker cannot see reads as missing
# work forever.
GONE = re.compile(r"gone from the Store|removed from the Store|delisted|confirmed 404"
                  r"|no longer resolve|returns? `?404|404 record-or-token-not-found", re.I)
OTHER_HANDLE = re.compile(r"`[\w.-]+/[\w.-]+`")

# Structural non-handles: a backticked repo path or a file.
NOT_HANDLE_EXT = re.compile(
    r"\.(md|py|json|db|sh|txt|js|ts|tsx|yml|yaml|html|png|jpg|svg|csv|xml|log|toml|ini|sql)$")
REPO_DIRS = {"bin", "actors", "site", "data", "state", "notes", "tasks", "logs", "secrets",
             "venv", "mail", "tmp", "docs", "src", "static", "templates", "content",
             # URL-path roots: `api/appdetails`, `v2/acts`, never Apify usernames.
             "api", "v2", "www", "http", "https"}
# Hand-added tokens shaped like a handle that are not Apify Actor handles. One line each,
# added after a sweep proves it -- same convention as COMPETITORS in check-competitor-claims.
KNOWN_NON_HANDLES = {
    "api.github.com/rate_limit",
    "data.uspto.gov/home",
    "developer.uspto.gov/ds-api",
    "fallback.era.nih.gov/systemUnavailable",
    "holidu.jobs.personio.de/xml",
    "device/enforcement.json",
    "drug/enforcement.json",
    "food/enforcement.json",
    # c2845 fleet run: MIME types, UAs, and code tokens, not owner/slug handles.
    "curl/8.5.0",
    "image/png",
    "application/json",
    "text/html",
    "python-requests/2.x",
    "try/catch",
    "tools/call",
    # c2845: this specific blog post's SUBJECT is GitHub-repo attribution from HN
    # comments, so it quotes real GitHub `owner/repo` pairs that are not Apify Actors
    # (and never will be) -- same pair is also quoted in hacker-news-scraper's README
    # as a worked example of the same scraper behavior.
    "omcljs/om",
    "reagent-project/reagent",
    "magomimmo/modern-cljs",
    "owner/repo",
    # c2845: Google News RSS query-param examples (`topic/<NAME>`, `geo/<CITY>`) quoted
    # in a blog post about the RSS endpoint's own URL shape, not an Apify handle.
    "topic/TECHNOLOGY",
    "topic/NOTATOPIC",
    "geo/Chicago",
    # c2920: Apify's own CSV array-export column names, quoted in eu-ted-tenders-scraper's
    # `flatten` input docs ("splits each into numbered `field/0`, `field/1` columns").
    # A digits-only second segment is never an Actor slug, but keep this as two hand-added
    # lines rather than a numeric-slug heuristic, per the one-line-each convention above.
    "field/0",
    "field/1",
}


def token():
    for line in open(os.path.join(ROOT, "secrets/env")):
        if line.startswith("APIFY_TOKEN"):
            return line.split("=", 1)[1].strip().strip('"')
    sys.exit("check-handle-liveness: no APIFY_TOKEN in secrets/env")


def sources(only=()):
    """Every doc that can name a competitor: Actor READMEs + published blog posts.

    Same glob as check-competitor-claims.sources() on purpose -- if one tool's corpus
    drifts from the other's, a handle can be 'covered' by neither. A filtered run proves
    nothing about the rest of the fleet, so --only refuses to be the run of record."""
    paths = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(ROOT, "actors/*/README.md"))
                   + glob.glob(os.path.join(ROOT, "site/content/blog/*.md")))
    if not only:
        return paths
    return [p for p in paths if any(o in os.path.relpath(p, ROOT) for o in only)]


# A backticked `host.tld/path` URL (`itunes.apple.com/lookup`,
# `okgov.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/okgovjobs`) is shaped exactly like a handle and 404s exactly
# like a delisted one -- 10 of the 128 404s in the first c2844 fleet sweep were these. It
# is NOT filtered up front, because `caffein.dev`, `gooyer.co`, `examsens.ai` and
# `insight.solutions` are real Apify usernames in our copy: a dots-in-owner filter would
# silently stop checking real rivals. Instead the lookup still runs, a 200 is a real live
# handle as before, and only a 404 on a domain-shaped owner is set aside -- reported in its
# own bucket, never silently, so a genuinely delisted `caffein.dev/...` is still visible.
DOMAIN_LIKE = re.compile(
    r"\.(com|org|net|gov|edu|io|dev|ai|co|uk|de|eu|jobs|me|info|app|xyz|cloud|tech|gl)$")


def is_handle(owner, slug):
    ident = f"{owner}/{slug}"
    if ident in KNOWN_NON_HANDLES or owner in REPO_DIRS or owner == OURS:
        return False
    return not NOT_HANDLE_EXT.search(slug)


def occurrences(only=()):
    """{ident: [(relpath, lineno, end_offset, text)]} for every real handle mention."""
    hits = {}
    for path in sources(only):
        name = os.path.relpath(path, ROOT)
        text = open(path).read()
        for m in HANDLE.finditer(text):
            owner, slug = m.groups()
            if not is_handle(owner, slug):
                continue
            lineno = text.count("\n", 0, m.start()) + 1
            hits.setdefault(f"{owner}/{slug}", []).append((name, lineno, m.end(), text))
    return hits


def status(ident, headers):
    """HTTP status of GET /v2/acts/<owner>~<slug>, retried once on any non-200.

    Cycles 1145/1148/1159: a single non-200 has repeatedly turned out to be a transient
    API hiccup on a listing a re-check moments later confirmed live. A delisting claim we
    print into a README has to survive two lookups."""
    u, n = ident.split("/", 1)
    url = f"https://api.apify.com/v2/acts/{u}~{n}"
    for attempt in (0, 1):
        try:
            code = httpx.get(url, headers=headers, timeout=30).status_code
        except httpx.HTTPError:
            code = -1
        if code == 200:
            return 200
        if attempt == 0:
            time.sleep(2)
    return code


def paragraph(text, pos):
    """The blank-line-to-blank-line paragraph containing pos (check-competitor-claims)."""
    start = text.rfind("\n\n", 0, pos)
    end = text.find("\n\n", pos)
    return text[(0 if start < 0 else start + 2):(len(text) if end < 0 else end)]


def classify(text, end):
    """"disclosed" | "shared" | "undisclosed" for one mention.

    check-competitor-claims 2091/2092's rule verbatim for "disclosed". "shared" is the
    group-disclosure case in the module docstring, and the test for it is PARAGRAPH-wide
    rather than window-forward because of the shape the c2844 smoke run actually found:
    `steam-reviews-scraper`'s 2026-10-28 paragraph opens with the 404 statement for two
    handles, then restates both further down under "Original note, kept for the method".
    The restatement has no notice after it at all, so a forward-only test calls an
    already-honest paragraph undisclosed. A paragraph-wide test cannot be trusted to say
    DISCLOSED (that is the c2092 hole), but it is exactly the right trigger for "a human
    should read this paragraph once"."""
    window = text[end:end + WINDOW]
    gone = GONE.search(window)
    if gone and not OTHER_HANDLE.search(window[:gone.start()]):
        return "disclosed"
    return "shared" if GONE.search(paragraph(text, end)) else "undisclosed"


if __name__ == "__main__":
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    only = tuple(args[i + 1] for i, a in enumerate(args) if a == "--only" and i + 1 < len(args))
    save = "--save" in args
    if only:
        print(f"NOTE: --only {' '.join(only)} -- PARTIAL sweep, {len(sources(only))} file(s). "
              f"Does NOT satisfy the fleet_checks.json cadence; a clean result here says "
              f"nothing about the other {len(sources()) - len(sources(only))} file(s).")
        if not sources(only):
            sys.exit("--only matched no file; pass a substring of actors/<slug>/README.md")

    headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {token()}"}
    hits = occurrences(only)
    idents = sorted(hits)
    print(f"check-handle-liveness: {len(idents)} unique competitor handle(s) across "
          f"{len(sources(only))} doc(s), {sum(len(v) for v in hits.values())} mention(s)")

    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=WORKERS) as pool:
        codes = dict(zip(idents, pool.map(lambda i: status(i, headers), idents)))

    # A handle can appear twice on one line (e.g. a price claim restating it); the work is
    # per line, not per match, so the same file:line:handle must not print twice. Where two
    # matches on one line disagree, the WEAKEST classification wins -- if any mention on
    # that line is undisclosed, the line still needs an edit.
    rank = {"undisclosed": 0, "shared": 1, "disclosed": 2}
    verdicts = {}
    anomalies, url_like = [], []
    for ident in idents:
        code = codes[ident]
        if code == 200:
            continue
        if code != 404:
            anomalies.append((ident, code))
            continue
        if DOMAIN_LIKE.search(ident.split("/")[0]):
            url_like.append(ident)
            continue
        for name, lineno, end, text in hits[ident]:
            key = (name, lineno, ident)
            v = classify(text, end)
            if key not in verdicts or rank[v] < rank[verdicts[key]]:
                verdicts[key] = v

    def bucket(kind):
        return sorted(k for k, v in verdicts.items() if v == kind)

    undisclosed, shared, already = bucket("undisclosed"), bucket("shared"), bucket("disclosed")
    for name, lineno, ident in undisclosed:
        print(f"UNDISCLOSED {name}:{lineno}  `{ident}` -- 404, no removal notice near the mention")
    for name, lineno, ident in shared:
        print(f"SHARED {name}:{lineno}  `{ident}` -- 404, notice nearby but another handle "
              f"intervenes; hand-read once")
    for name, lineno, ident in already:
        print(f"DISCLOSED {name}:{lineno}  `{ident}` -- 404, already disclosed in the text")
    for ident in url_like:
        print(f"URL-LIKE `{ident}` -- 404 on a domain-shaped owner; read as a URL in our copy, "
              f"not a handle. NOT work. Add to KNOWN_NON_HANDLES if you confirm it.")
    for ident, code in anomalies:
        print(f"ANOMALY `{ident}` -- HTTP {code} twice; NOT treated as delisted, re-check by hand")

    live = sum(1 for c in codes.values() if c == 200)
    dead = sum(1 for c in codes.values() if c == 404)
    print(f"\n{len(idents)} handle(s): {live} live, {dead} delisted (404) of which "
          f"{len(url_like)} are URL-shaped and set aside, {len(anomalies)} anomal(y/ies).")
    print(f"{len(undisclosed)} line(s) need a disclosure note, {len(shared)} to hand-read "
          f"(group disclosure), {len(already)} already disclosed.")

    if save:
        out = os.path.join(ROOT, "state/handle_liveness.json")
        with open(out, "w") as f:
            json.dump({
                "_why": "Last check-handle-liveness sweep. UNDISCLOSED is the work queue: "
                        "each mention needs the standard inline delisted note + a past-tensed "
                        "claim, one Actor (one build) at a time.",
                "swept": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d"),
                "partial_only": list(only),
                "handles": len(idents), "live": live, "delisted": dead,
                "undisclosed": [{"file": n, "line": l, "handle": i} for n, l, i in undisclosed],
                "shared": [{"file": n, "line": l, "handle": i} for n, l, i in shared],
                "disclosed": [{"file": n, "line": l, "handle": i} for n, l, i in already],
                "url_like_404": url_like,
                "anomalies": [{"handle": i, "code": c} for i, c in anomalies],
            }, f, indent=1)
            f.write("\n")
        print(f"wrote {os.path.relpath(out, ROOT)}")
    sys.exit(1 if undisclosed else 0)
